+# Release blocked: libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz checksum mismatch
+
+## What is wrong
+
+`SHA256SUMS` and the artifact on disk disagree for one file:
+
+| file | expected (SHA256SUMS) | actual (artifact) |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` | `7BEE020AC21EFB3831D920AF0B8C5EFE8D44CDF227AB94847F509188420356BD` | `fde9b2575edbefb297fa7d93738b4bb1f09b7f777d1723b90194ad53cd9bc9ce` |
+
+`cli-2.0.1.zip` and `docs-2026.10.tar.gz` both match the manifest.
+
+## Why it cannot be fixed in this checkout
+
+The repository contains only the bytes in `artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz`; there is no original, source tree, build recipe, or previous revision from which to regenerate it. `git log`/`git fsck` show a single commit with no other version of the artifact or manifest.
+
+The only locally possible ways to make the check pass would be to:
+
+- edit `SHA256SUMS` to the hash of the current file, or
+- replace the artifact with one matching the manifest.
+
+Both would fake the integrity check rather than fix the release input. Either makes the release job green while shipping bytes that were never verified against the published checksum, so neither was done.
+
+## What was fixed
+
+`verify.py` had two independent bugs, now fixed:
+
+1. It read artifacts in text mode and encoded the decoded string, which crashes on binary files (`UnicodeDecodeError`). It now reads in binary mode.
+2. It compared the computed lowercase hex digest to the manifest entry case-sensitively, so valid uppercase manifests were reported as mismatches. Comparison is now case-insensitive.
+
+With those fixes, `test_binary_files` and `test_reports_missing` pass, and `verify.py` correctly reports exactly `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` as bad.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+Provide the genuine `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` that matches the published hash (or regenerate it from its source), then re-run `python3 verify.py`. If the current file is known-good and the manifest is wrong, the checksum must be corrected through the release/signing process with confirmation of the artifact's provenance — not by silently rewriting `SHA256SUMS` here.
